he Pre-Deployment Polish Checklist

Before you host this on the internet (e.g., Vercel for Frontend, Render/Railway for Backend), you must change these configurations:

A. Remove the Hardcoded "123456" OTP (Backend)
Right now, your AuthService.java accepts "123456" as a universal password bypass.

    Production Move: You will need to integrate a service like Twilio (for SMS) or JavaMailSender / SendGrid (for Emails). You will generate a random 6-digit number (int)(Math.random() * 900000) + 100000, save it to the database with a 5-minute expiration time, and verify against that database record instead of the hardcoded string.

B. Externalize your API Base URL (Frontend)
In all your React components, you have const API_BASE_URL = 'http://localhost:8081/api/v1';.

    Production Move: Create a .env file in your React project:
    Code snippet

    VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8081/api/v1

    And change your React files to use const API_BASE_URL = import.meta.env.VITE_API_BASE_URL;. When you deploy to Vercel, you will swap that URL to your real hosted backend link.

C. Remove System.out.println and Enable Proper Logging
Ensure your backend isn't printing sensitive data to the console. Spring Boot's built-in SLF4J (@Slf4j from Lombok) should be used so you can track errors securely.

D. Clear LocalStorage on Token Expiration (Frontend)
Since your JWT token is set to expire after 24 hours, ensure your frontend AuthContext has an interceptor that automatically logs the user out and clears localStorage.removeItem('token') if the backend returns a 401 Unauthorized status.